## Why each additional element was added

**3 firewalls:** Added to filter network traffic in and out of each
machine. Firewall 1 sits in front of the load balancer and blocks
unauthorized incoming internet traffic. Firewalls 2 and 3 protect each
server and only allow traffic coming from the load balancer on the
expected ports.

**1 SSL certificate:** Added to serve www.foobar.com over HTTPS. If
someone intercepts the traffic between the user and the load balancer,
the data is encrypted and cannot be read. This protects passwords,
session tokens, and all sensitive data in transit.

**3 monitoring clients:** Added to collect data from each machine (load
balancer + 2 servers) and send it to a monitoring service (Sumologic).
This allows the team to detect problems, measure performance, and trigger
alerts automatically.

## What firewalls are for

A firewall filters network traffic going in and out of a machine. It
applies a set of rules to decide which connections are allowed and which
are blocked. For example, it can block all traffic except HTTPS on port
443 from the internet, and only allow the load balancer to talk to the
web servers on port 80 internally.

## Why traffic is served over HTTPS

HTTPS encrypts the traffic between the user's browser and the server
using SSL/TLS. If someone intercepts the connection (man-in-the-middle
attack), they only see encrypted data that they cannot read. Without
HTTPS, passwords, personal data, and session tokens travel in plain text
and can be stolen.

## What monitoring is used for

Monitoring is used to check if something is broken or slow. It watches
server health metrics (CPU usage, RAM, disk space, response time, error
rate) and triggers alerts when something goes out of normal range. It
allows the team to detect and fix problems before users notice them.

## How the monitoring tool collects data

The monitoring setup is composed of a lightweight agent (client) installed
on each server. This agent continuously collects data from the machine
(system metrics, application logs, request counts) and sends it to the
monitoring service (Sumologic) over the network. Sumologic stores,
visualizes, and analyzes this data and can trigger alerts based on
configured thresholds.

## How to monitor web server QPS (Queries Per Second)

To monitor QPS on the Nginx web server, configure the monitoring agent to
read and parse the Nginx access logs or to collect the Nginx stub status
metrics (using ngx_http_stub_status_module). The agent sends the request
count per second to Sumologic. In Sumologic, create a dashboard with a
QPS metric and set up an alert that triggers if QPS exceeds a defined
threshold, indicating abnormal traffic or a potential attack.

## Issues with this infrastructure

### SSL termination at the load balancer level

SSL is terminated at the load balancer. This means the load balancer
decrypts the traffic and forwards it to the web servers in plain HTTP.
The traffic between the load balancer and the web servers is unencrypted.
If an attacker gains access to the internal network, they can read all
the traffic between the load balancer and the servers. To fix this, SSL
should also be used between the load balancer and the web servers
(end-to-end encryption).

The load balancer is also still a single point of failure. If it goes
down, the entire website becomes unreachable.

### Only one MySQL server capable of accepting writes

Only the Primary MySQL node accepts write operations. If the Primary goes
down, the application cannot write to the database anymore (no new users,
no new orders, no updates) until the Primary is restored or the Replica
is manually promoted to Primary. This is a critical availability risk for
any write-heavy application.

### Servers with all the same components

Having servers where each one contains a database, a web server, and an
application server together creates two problems.

First, each component has a different resource consumption pattern. The
database might need more memory and disk while the application server
needs more CPU. Mixing them on the same machine means they compete for
resources and cannot be scaled independently. We might need more database
servers than application servers, but this architecture does not allow
that.

Second, when maintenance is needed on one component (for example,
restarting Nginx to deploy new code), it affects all other components
running on the same server (the application server and the database both
become temporarily unreachable). Separating components onto dedicated
servers would allow independent maintenance without collateral impact.